<Base
  title="Research Program · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="A research program on how structured meaning is represented, processed, and learned, connecting formal semantics with psycholinguistic and computational evidence."
>
  <article class="rp">
    <h1 class="rp-h1">Research Program</h1>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Linguistic meaning and inference</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        My work asks how linguistic meaning guides later inference, and how to identify the
        information used by people and language models. The projects approach particular parts of
        this question through formal analysis, model evaluation, and proposed behavioural work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">From analysis to predictions</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Formal semantics offers explicit alternatives rather than a general claim that a system
        “understands” an expression. For each account, the questions are what should remain possible
        after an utterance, which inferences it should license, and what later evidence should change.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Those predictions determine the experiment. Depending on the question, the relevant evidence
        may come from behavioural work, controlled model training, diagnostic evaluation, or
        intervention. The method serves the linguistic contrast; it does not define the research
        identity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Human cognition</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Psycholinguistic evidence can show whether a theoretically defined distinction affects
        comprehension, later reasoning, or learning. My master’s training in cognitive psychology,
        experimental design, and statistics provides the basis for turning a semantic contrast into
        controlled behavioural predictions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Computational learning and representation</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Language models make a different set of questions tractable: what can be learned from a
        specified input distribution, whether learning generalises beyond surface cues, and which
        internal representations are causally involved in a controlled behaviour. Training,
        evaluation, and intervention are useful here because each exposes a different part of the
        learning process.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Evidence without substitution</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Human and model studies do not answer the same question. Behavioural evidence concerns how
        people process or learn a distinction; controlled model studies concern what a particular
        learner can acquire from particular input. Comparing them can clarify hypotheses, but a
        language model is not a proxy for a person and a human result does not establish what a model
        has learned.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">How the projects connect</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <a href="/research/cross-lingual-protobias">Cross-lingual ProtoBias</a> is the completed
        empirical foundation: it tests whether models follow explicit semantic constraints when
        these conflict with typicality, and whether their choices change across prompt languages.
        This provides behavioural evidence about how linguistic information interacts with prior
        expectations. That audit also produced a question it could not answer with its own
        design, and <a href="/research/typicality-in-referent-choice">Typicality in referent
        choice</a> is the answer to it: the benchmark scores a forced choice between two images,
        but a wrong answer is ambiguous between a description that failed to override typicality
        and a description that never ruled the other candidate out. The paper separates the three
        conditions the design presupposes but does not itself distinguish, and states what a forced
        choice can and cannot report. It is published as a working paper
        (<a href="https://lingbuzz.net/lingbuzz/010343" rel="noopener">lingbuzz/010343</a>) and
        reports no new data. <a href="/research/latent-control-states">Latent Control States</a>
        provides training in mechanistic interpretability: using controlled behavioural contrasts
        to develop hypotheses about model decisions, then designing internal interventions to test
        which representations and computations contribute to an answer. This method can inform
        future studies of linguistic meaning in models. Behavioural pilots are in place; the
        mechanistic tests are still being developed.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <a href="/research/three-ways">Three Ways of Leaving <em>p</em> Unsettled</a> develops the
        theoretical side: what apparently similar epistemic expressions contribute to discourse.
        A <a href="/research/learnability">human-focused thesis</a>, currently under discussion,
        could test whether those differences affect later access to and use of a possibility.
        This is a direct connection between linguistic analysis and a psychological question.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Scope and progress</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        These projects have different goals and levels of completion. Their connection is a recurring
        concern with linguistic contrasts, competing explanations, and what a particular measurement
        establishes. Each study needs a question that can be answered within its own scope. Human
        processing, formal interpretation, and computational learning can inform one another without
        all becoming requirements of a single thesis.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="rp-foot"><a href="/research">See how this program is being developed in the current work →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

